/** Question:
We call an array A = [A1, A2, . . . , AM] of M integers good if Ai − Ai−1 ≤ K for all 1 < i ≤ M.
Given an array of integers A, C is said to be a subsequence of A if it is possible to delete some (possibly
none) elements from A to form C, without changing the order of the remaining elements. For example,
the non-empty subsequences of [1, 1, 3] are [1], [1], [3], [1, 1], [1, 3], [1, 3] and [1, 1, 3].
You are given a non-decreasing array A of N integers and the parameter K. Your task is to find
the number of non-empty subsequences of A that are good. Notice that the same subsequence may
arise multiple times in the array A. Each subsequence should be counted as many times as it appears
in A.
For example, for the input N = 3, K = 1, A = [1, 1, 3], there are 4 good non-empty subsequences,
which are [1], [1], [3] and [1, 1].
(a) N = 8, K = 0, A = [1, 1, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4, 4]
(b) N = 8, K = 4, A = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]
(c) N = 15, K = 9, A = [1, 1, 2, 3, 4, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 10, 10, 11, 12, 14]
**/

// Solution ( My First DP Solution )
import java.util.*;
public class good_sub {
   // ZIO Problem 2
	public static void main(String args[]) {
		
		// This is a classic DP Problem. creds to the IITGn professor that took time to explain this trivial ( impossibly difficult for me ) problem
		
		// There's many different levels to this. K is a number by which successive elements should differ at most by. 
		// how to solve this: for each i, calculate the number of GOOD subsequences at that i ( "ending" cus of atmost). storing this in d[i]
		// hence, the answer will be sum of subsequences ending at all i. 
		// while hand computing, what you realize is: you're just appending that new value ( new last posn ) to all the previous subsequences in which appending is valid 
		// i.e Ai - Aj doesnt grown bigger than k. this, and you're introducing one more value per i, cus of the singleton set for that i. 
		
		// so it reduces to: d[i] = (sum from d[j] to d[i-1], where j is the least value that satisfies A[i] - A[j] <= k ) + 1. 
		Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
		System.out.println("Enter size of Good sorted array");
		int n = sc.nextInt();
		int A[] = new int[n];
	    System.out.println("Enter Array");
	    for ( int i = 0 ; i < n; i ++) {
	    	A[i] = sc.nextInt();
	    	
	    }
	    System.out.println("Ënter value by which successive elements must at most differ by");
	    int k = sc.nextInt();
	    
	    
	    int d[] = new int[n]; // to store all the good subsequences ending at i. Final ans. to problem is d[0] + d[1] + .. + d[n-1] ( n-1 cus java is zero indexed )
	    
	    // in this question, given all the conditions, d[0] = 1 always as singleton set is always correct no matter what k.
	    d[0] = 1;
	    
	    int j = 0;
	    for ( int i = 1; i < n; i ++) { // filling up the d[i] array
	    	// main concern is to get j. so, for each i, we iterate through the array, i = 0 to i = i -1. if no j is found, d[i] = 0 + 1 = 1
	    	for (  j = 0; j <= i; j ++) {
	    		if (A[i] - A[j] <= k ) { 
	    			break; // we have the j value
	    		}
	    		
	    	}
	    	if(j == i ) {
	    		// meaning no such j exists
	    		d[i] = 1;
	    	}
	    	else {
	    		for ( int a = j; a < i; a ++) { // summing from d[j] to d[i-1] 
	    			d[i] += d[a];
	    		}
	    		d[i] ++;
	    	}
	    	
	    }
	    
	    int ans = 0;
	    for ( int i = 0; i < n; i ++) {
	    	ans += d[i];
	    }
	    
	    System.out.println("Number of GOOD Subsequences: " + ans);
	    
	    }
	}

